. ../../testutils.sh

# What our modules take from each other, by concepts (Lint.Concept): a
# type with its constructors, fields, implementations and the functions
# about it is one concept; anything else is one of its own.
#
# precision: of the concepts an import offers, the share used. Dump,
# Sizes, Places and Bits each import Heap.Header's three concepts and use
# one or two of them, Norms one of Geo.Point's two.
#
# separation: Heap.Header's concepts fall in two pieces no importer needs
# together, `Header` (Dump, Sizes) and `Part` with `wordBits` (Places,
# Bits, which uses both), each of 20 lines or more: a split would cost no
# importer anything. Heap.Glued's two functions are used apart too, but
# both use a private helper, which a split would have to export: one
# piece.
#
# distinction also counts a member too small to stand alone, under 20
# lines: Heap.Left and Heap.Right are, and so are Ops.Add and Ops.Sub.
#
# Bulk leaves itself out of precision, which names it.
#
# distinction: Heap.Left and Heap.Right are always imported together, by
# the same two modules: probably one module split in two, two of Heap's
# three members. Ops.Add and Ops.Sub are too, all of Ops: a group that
# could be one module, a candidate to collapse.
#
# The three components read the TTCs the build writes, so the fixtures are
# built first.
idris2 --build fixtures.ipkg > /dev/null
../../../tools/lint/build/exec/idris2-native-lint --resolved --health --offenders 3 . \
  | awk '/^    [a-z]/ { show = ($1 == "precision" || $1 == "separation" || $1 == "distinction") } show'
rm -rf build
